/**
 * @page cmd_svc_table Services and Commands Registration
 *
 * @brief Overview of service initialization and shell command registration.
 *
 * ## Services Registration
 *
 * Services are registered using the @c _SERVICE and @c _SERVICE_INIT (Optional) macros.
 *
 * The @c _SERVICE macro declares a service and makes it available to the
 * kernel's service infrastructure.
 *
 * @code
 * _SERVICE(svc_name, service_name);
 * @endcode
 *
 * ## Services initialization
 *
 * The @c _SERVICE_INIT macro associates an initialization function with a
 * service. The function is called as part of the service initialization
 * process.
 *
 * @code
 * _SERVICE_INIT(svc_intent_name, service_init_function);
 * @endcode
 *
 * ## Command Registration
 *
 * Shell commands are registered using the @c _SHELL_COMMAND macro.
 *
 * The @c _SHELL_COMMAND macro registers a command so that it can be invoked
 * from the kernel shell.
 *
 * @code
 * _SHELL_COMMAND(command_name, command_handler);
 * @endcode
 *
 * ## Registration Workflow
 *
 * The registration macros provide a declarative way to expose services and
 * commands to the kernel:
 *
 * - @c _SERVICE declares a service.
 * - @c _SERVICE_INIT associates an initialization function with a service.
 * - @c _SHELL_COMMAND registers a shell command and its handler.
 *
 * @note The exact macro arguments, handler signatures, and initialization
 *       order are defined by their implementations in the kernel headers.
 *
 * @see \ref kernel_arch
 * @see \ref kernel_stdio
 * @see \ref kernel_sgls
 * @see \ref cmd_list
 */